<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Violeta Chamorro</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1929">1929</a>–<a href="/years/2025">2025</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Violeta Barrios de Chamorro</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Violeta Barrios de Chamorro was a Nicaraguan publisher and politician who was president of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicaragua">Nicaragua</a> from 1990 to 1997, the first woman elected president in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>. The widow of Pedro Joaquín Chamorro, she briefly sat on the Sandinista junta in 1979–1980 before leading the opposition coalition that defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daniel-ortega">Daniel Ortega</a> in February 1990. Her government ended the Contra war, reduced the army and stabilised the economy.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/violeta-chamorro.jpg" alt="Violeta Chamorro" width="320" height="386" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: EC - Audiovisual Service · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Violeta_Chamorro_1991.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Violeta Barrios Torres was born on 18 October 1929 in Rivas, near the Costa Rican border, into a wealthy and conservative family with large landholdings and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a>. She attended school in Rivas, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/granada">Granada</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/managua">Managua</a> and then American schools in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/texas">Texas</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virginia">Virginia</a>, returning home in June 1947 when her father was diagnosed with terminal lung cancer. She met <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pedro-joaquin-chamorro-cardenal">Pedro Joaquín Chamorro Cardenal</a> in 1949, and they married in December 1950 and had five children. In 1952 he inherited the newspaper La Prensa, which became a voice of opposition to the Somoza regime, and he was frequently jailed or exiled; she followed him abroad or visited him in jail for about a decade.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>After her husband's assassination in 1978 she took over La Prensa. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daniel-ortega">Daniel Ortega</a> led the Sandinista guerrillas into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/managua">Managua</a> in July 1979, she was with them and represented the Democratic Union of Liberation on the Junta of National Reconstruction. She resigned on 19 April 1980, when the junta moved in a more radical direction and signed agreements with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>, and returned to the newspaper, which went on criticising the government despite threats and forced shutdowns.</p><p>From 1987 a group of fourteen parties worked together, and by 1989 Ortega had agreed to free, monitored elections. The National Opposition Union chose Chamorro as its candidate after five rounds of voting. Her platform promised to end the civil war and military service, and she won the election on 25 February 1990 despite polls favouring Ortega.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>Chamorro was sworn in on 25 April 1990, the first time in more than five decades that a government had peacefully surrendered power to the opposition. She abolished conscription on the day she took office, cut the army by half to three quarters within weeks and demobilised the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/contras">Contras</a>. She also granted unconditional amnesties for political crimes. Her administration tackled hyperinflation that had at one point exceeded 13,000 per cent, with a programme of privatisation and a new currency, the córdoba oro.</p><p>In 1995 a constitutional crisis arose over reforms that reduced presidential power, which she at first refused to publish. She left office on 10 January 1997, and in July 1997 founded a foundation to support peace initiatives. She moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/costa-rica">Costa Rica</a> in October 2023 and died in San José on 14 June 2025, aged 95.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Violeta Chamorro” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Under the Esquipulas peace process of 1987 the Sandinistas held free elections, and in February 1990 <b>Violeta Barrios de Chamorro</b>, Pedro Joaquín’s widow, defeated Daniel Ortega and ended the war.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-nicaragua">Republic of Nicaragua · Into the Present</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Rivas, in what is now <a href="/countries/nicaragua">Nicaragua</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-jose-costa-rica">San José</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/costa-rica">Costa Rica</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=11.44,-85.83&amp;year=1991" aria-label="Rivas on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Violeta Chamorro"><image href="/maps/globes/g27000.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5349 4028h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5349 4028h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=11.44,-85.83&amp;year=1991">What was here in 1991? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
